  "summary": "Embroidery technique incorporating small mirrors into vibrant geometric patterns.",
  "description": "Features reflective mirrors (abhla) stitched into elaborate folk designs using chain, satin, and buttonhole stitches; created by nomadic and pastoral communities of Kutch as practical decoration that also wards off evil spirits. The mirrors symbolize water and light, reflecting the harsh desert environment while creating dazzling visual effects.",
  "culturalContext": "Shisha embroidery originated in the 17th century in India. People in lower classes mimicked the jeweled garments of the wealthy by decorating fabric with silver beetles' wings and chips of mica. When a process for manufacturing tiny mirror discs was developed during the Mughal Empire, these tiny mirrors or shisha were swiftly adopted for fabric embellishment.\n\nContemporary shisha work typically makes use of mass-produced, machine-cut glass shisha with a silvered backing. Today most craft stores in the South Asia carry small mirrors purchasable for use in embroidery, which come in varying shapes and sizes.",
